The verifier error is clear: `helper_a` calls itself recursively, which the BPF verifier does not allow. The BPF verifier requires an acyclic call graph to bound stack depth.

The fix is to rewrite the recursion as a bounded loop within `helper_a`. Looking at the logic:

```
r1 += 1;
r0 = r1;
if r1 < 4 goto l0_%=;
exit;
l0_%=: call helper_a;
exit;
```

This increments r1, sets r0 = r1, then if r1 < 4, calls helper_a again. This is essentially a loop that increments r1 until r1 >= 4.


When `entry` calls `helper_a` with r1 = 0, the function increments r1 to 1, then keeps recursing and incrementing until r1 reaches 4, at which point it exits. I can unroll this into an inline loop that performs the same increments without the recursive calls.

Writing loop unroll... Writing BPF assembly code... Flipping the condition so the loop jumps back to the start if r1 is less than 4 and exits once it reaches 4 should work, since modern BPF kernels support bounded loops and this codebase is already using contemporary BPF features.

